WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.230
- I'm really excited to
welcome Murray Hiebert

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:10.490
to today's Brown Bag

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:12.310
which will be the last
one at the semester.

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:14.750
We've had a very dynamic session

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:17.044
of Brown Bags this semester

00:00:17.044 --> 00:00:20.123
with people from all over
the world Zooming in.

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:23.600
Murray is currently a senior associate

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.160
of the Southeast Asia program

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:28.050
at the Center for Strategic
and International Studies,

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.910
C.S.I.S. in Washington, D.C.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:32.540
and he has recently completed a book

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:35.360
about China's involvement
in Southeast Asia,

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.848
which we will be hearing about today.

00:00:37.848 --> 00:00:40.410
I'm especially excited
to have him here today

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:42.080
because it brings me back

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:45.370
to my first year in
graduate school at Cornell

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:48.830
when I was just beginning my
academic study of Vietnam.

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:52.070
I've been working in Vietnam
for over 20 years now,

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:55.050
but back then in the
Kroch Library at Cornell,

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:56.790
his book "Chasing the Tigers:

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.830
A Portrait of the New Vietnam",

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:01.150
which was published in 1996,

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:05.040
built from his firsthand
observations in the early 1990s

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:06.350
when he was based in Hanoi

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:09.540
and writing for the Far
Eastern Economic Review,

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:10.600
which at that time,

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:12.380
those of you who have been studying

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:14.230
Southeast Asia for a long time,

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:17.270
was one of the most important
sources of serious journalism

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:18.560
for all of Southeast Asia

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.610
and other parts of Asia as well.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.040
Some of you probably remember

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:25.360
our own recently retired
colleague Nayan Chanda,

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.360
who also worked for the Far
Eastern Economic Review.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:30.330
And like Nayan, Murray Hiebert

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:34.210
was part of an important
cohort of embedded journalists

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:37.240
who were offering on the
ground reporting of Vietnam

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.610
during important post-war periods.

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:42.240
And his Vietnam books
are especially important

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.650
for the portraits they
offer of the early 1990s

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:46.850
when some of the changes

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:50.100
associated with Vietnam's (mumbles) period

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:51.999
and the post socialist reforms

00:01:51.999 --> 00:01:54.460
were beginning to come into effect.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:56.640
And it was a time when society in Vietnam

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.430
was rapidly transforming

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:01.270
economically, politically and socially.

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:03.940
And Murray Hiebert was
one of the few people

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:05.460
who was really on the ground

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.220
writing about all those transformations.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:09.480
It's been more than 20 years

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:12.317
since I first read that
book in Cornell's library

00:02:12.317 --> 00:02:14.300
and I'm really eager

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.910
to be able to meet
Murray for the first time

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:18.200
and to hear what he has to say

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.140
about China's involvement
in Southeast Asia.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.840
So please join me in
welcoming Murray Hiebert

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.840
to our Zoom edition Brown Bag series.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:28.070
His talk today,

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:29.960
like his newly published book,

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.890
is entitled "Under Beijing's Shadow:

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:35.620
Southeast Asia's China Challenge."

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:36.610
Murray?

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:40.130
- Great, thanks a lot for
that kind introduction, Eric,

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:43.853
and you've really flashed
me back to the old days.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:46.600
Those were the days actually

00:02:48.174 --> 00:02:50.060
in journalism in Southeast Asia,

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:52.294
or in a lot of parts of Asia,

00:02:52.294 --> 00:02:54.410
'cause foreign journalists were,

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:55.700
there was quite a few of them,

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:57.930
and we had quite a few outlets.

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:00.250
On the Far Eastern Economic Review,

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:03.670
I was lucky to be working
for them and they,

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:07.640
yeah, they provided a lot
of the early reporting

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:10.430
that was coming out of
countries like Vietnam

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:12.230
that was going through a transition.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.064
Now of course the media

00:03:15.064 --> 00:03:18.790
has really shrunk down
the number of journalists.

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:21.500
The Far Eastern Economic
Review no longer exists.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.510
It died in 2004.

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:25.460
It was owned by Dow Jones

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:28.200
that really didn't feel like
they could carry it anymore

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.790
when advertising was getting hammered.

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:35.790
Yeah so this, my new book
is about, like you said,

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:40.340
how Southeast Asia is
responding to China's rise.

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:44.740
So what I'll do is I'll try to give some

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:46.610
maybe in about half our time here,

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:50.700
give an overview of the
key themes of the book.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:55.182
I'll try to intersperse examples
from different countries,

00:03:55.182 --> 00:04:00.182
because one thing that
is quite quite clear

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.720
when you start doing
this research and writing

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:08.630
is that Southeast Asian countries

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:11.400
are all responding to
China very differently,

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.170
depending on how big they are,

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:16.683
how close they are to
China, their politics,

00:04:17.689 --> 00:04:19.933
those kinds of dynamics,

00:04:20.831 --> 00:04:25.220
and their history with China
is also very important.

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:28.080
But it's fair to say
that all the countries

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.140
really do respond to China,

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:35.140
see China as both a
challenge and an opportunity.

00:04:35.186 --> 00:04:37.590
The opportunity is that they think China

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:39.630
is gonna yank 'em up by their bootstraps

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:42.660
and help their economies develop,

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:44.570
which is in fact true.

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:48.932
All the countries have
benefited immensely.

00:04:48.932 --> 00:04:53.400
And then the challenge
is a considerable concern

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:56.250
about what it means for their
sovereignty going forward,

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:59.410
especially the countries
on the border of China,

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:03.600
which is Myanmar, Laos and Vietnam.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:08.120
So I go through, I do
it country by country.

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:10.250
That's not always the most
creative way to do it,

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:14.760
but you try to go themes
and it really doesn't work

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:17.630
because the themes are so different.

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:19.950
The issues you need to
cover in each country

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:21.080
is so different.

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:26.080
But I do look at China's
different tools that it brings.

00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:29.550
It brings soft power,

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:31.610
including economic cooperation,

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:35.050
cultural exchanges,

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:37.240
Mandarin language training,

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:38.440
more hard power,

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:41.000
that includes threats
in the South China Sea,

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:43.170
economic pressures on countries

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:45.567
that don't do what China wants,

00:05:45.567 --> 00:05:49.390
as well as military sales
and military exchanges.

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:53.170
And sometimes it even gets into
considerably sharper power,

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:57.150
like especially dealing
with its communist neighbor,

00:05:57.150 --> 00:05:58.740
biggest communist neighbor immediately

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:00.589
to the south, Vietnam,

00:06:00.589 --> 00:06:05.363
with which it has had
considerable disputes for decades,

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:10.860
but really in the recent
years a lot of pressure

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:13.950
on Vietnam's oil and gas exploration.

00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:17.213
I'll get into that later.

00:06:19.273 --> 00:06:21.800
The economic cooperation, as I mentioned,

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:23.363
has really exploded.

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:27.290
China's two-way trade

00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:31.480
with the 10 countries of Southeast Asia

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:35.250
was 60 billion last year,

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:39.070
excuse me, 600 billion last year,

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:43.002
which is double the US trade
figures with the region

00:06:43.002 --> 00:06:45.886
and it's up tremendously

00:06:45.886 --> 00:06:50.670
since China started opening up

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:53.470
in the late '70s and early '80s.

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:58.470
A lot of the economic benefit
also comes from tourism.

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:00.870
It's hard to get accurate numbers,

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:05.497
but there were an estimated
60 million Chinese tourists

00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:10.291
that visited the Southeast
Asian countries last year.

00:07:10.291 --> 00:07:13.660
That's obviously gonna be
down tremendously this year

00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:14.610
because of COVID.

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:16.840
The numbers will be really small

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:20.530
and it really is hurting
countries that rely on tourism,

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:23.410
like Thailand, for example.

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:27.540
And then the other big way

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:30.220
that countries look to China

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:32.190
is the Belt and Road Initiative.

00:07:32.190 --> 00:07:37.190
This is China's big signature
economic development project

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:44.860
that it's looking to build
globally railroads and dams

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:48.520
and highways and bridges
and power stations

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:49.830
and you name it.

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:53.520
They even include free trade agreements

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:56.940
or trade agreements in the Belt and Road.

00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:59.959
But China has really had trouble,

00:07:59.959 --> 00:08:04.610
it's had real difficulties
getting this thing off the road,

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:06.073
off the ground.

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:15.720
Every situation is really complex.

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:19.990
Even tiny Laos that
agreed to build a railroad

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:21.960
negotiated for five years.

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:22.793
You wouldn't have thought

00:08:22.793 --> 00:08:25.860
that they would be able to stand
up to China for five years,

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:29.210
but they were negotiating
over the interest rates

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:32.080
and how much Laos would contribute.

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:34.770
China starts by wanting you

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:37.270
to pay for your whole project yourself

00:08:37.270 --> 00:08:41.080
and Laos, that would
have been half their GDP,

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:43.386
and so they really fought back.

00:08:43.386 --> 00:08:46.610
China also wants land on
both sides of the railroad

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:47.560
so that they can,

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:51.793
you know, their business
people can make money.

00:08:53.165 --> 00:08:55.153
And finally, they did agree,

00:08:56.017 --> 00:08:58.960
and the railroad is now cranking along.

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:00.610
It'll be finished,

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:02.700
it was to be finished
at the end of next year,

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:07.700
but it might slip into 2022
because of the slowdown

00:09:07.871 --> 00:09:10.150
resulting from COVID.

00:09:10.150 --> 00:09:13.560
Thailand, which needs to
build the next railroad

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:15.340
so this thing keeps moving

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:19.080
all the way from Koon Ming to Singapore

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:22.170
has been negotiating since 2014

00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:25.400
when China thought with
the coup government,

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:28.960
the Prayut government would be able,

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:31.970
that they would be really
interested in doing this

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:34.950
because they were getting
a lot of isolation,

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:36.630
they were getting some sanctions

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:41.443
and other pressure from the
US and the European Union,

00:09:43.106 --> 00:09:47.173
but the Thai's and the Chinese
have had over 30 rounds

00:09:48.333 --> 00:09:52.130
of talks and they still haven't agreed.

00:09:52.130 --> 00:09:54.670
And there, the negotiations

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:57.250
are over slightly more complicated issues

00:09:57.250 --> 00:09:59.020
than they were in Laos.

00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:01.830
The Thai's say, "Well, we
don't need your money."

00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:03.510
Chinese money is not cheap.

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:07.730
They start usually from the EXIM bank,

00:10:07.730 --> 00:10:10.940
the Export-Import Bank or
the China Development Bank

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:14.131
usually start around
four and a half percent.

00:10:14.131 --> 00:10:17.170
These are both money-making institutions.

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:19.150
They're not aid organizations,

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:22.693
let's say, like, US EXIM or Japanese EXIM.

00:10:26.385 --> 00:10:29.010
So the Thai's said,
"Well we don't need that,

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:29.843
we don't need money.

00:10:29.843 --> 00:10:32.350
We can get money on the
international market."

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:35.210
They also said, "We don't
need your engineers."

00:10:35.210 --> 00:10:37.440
China likes to send lots of workers.

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:39.000
We don't need your engineers.

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:40.750
We got a lot of engineers of our own,

00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:44.936
and we don't necessarily
want your technology.

00:10:44.936 --> 00:10:48.930
There's German and there's
Japanese and other technology.

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:51.030
And so they're still
fussing around over this.

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:53.410
I don't know what's gonna happen.

00:10:53.410 --> 00:10:57.320
They did finally agree on
building a 2.5 kilometer,

00:10:57.320 --> 00:10:59.090
get this, 2.5 kilometers

00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:03.828
out of several hundred
kilometers of as a pilot project.

00:11:03.828 --> 00:11:05.780
And then in Indonesia,

00:11:05.780 --> 00:11:07.140
they also built a railroad,

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:10.440
but it's the high speed train
from Jakarta to Bandung.

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:13.390
It's about 150 kilometers.

00:11:13.390 --> 00:11:17.617
There, the thing is also
moving slowly since 2015,

00:11:17.617 --> 00:11:21.203
when it was agreed upon,

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:25.670
partially because of the
difficulties that China,

00:11:25.670 --> 00:11:28.780
Chinese companies and
their Indonesian partners

00:11:28.780 --> 00:11:30.330
have in getting land.

00:11:30.330 --> 00:11:33.600
So really absolutely every place.

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:36.200
And then actually there's Myanmar

00:11:36.200 --> 00:11:39.230
is also another interesting example.

00:11:39.230 --> 00:11:42.230
You would think that after they,

00:11:42.230 --> 00:11:44.720
Myanmar, the Aung San Suu Kyi government

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:48.550
was involved in kicking
out the Rohingya in 2017

00:11:48.550 --> 00:11:53.270
and early 2018 resulted in a
lot of isolation from the West.

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:58.220
You would think they would
want to move ahead with China,

00:11:58.220 --> 00:12:03.220
but it's, China has been
negotiating over the Chukwu Dam,

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:08.420
which is on the Western side
of Myanmar on the Indian Ocean.

00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:12.210
China has two pipelines,

00:12:12.210 --> 00:12:14.690
an oil and a gas pipeline
leading there already,

00:12:14.690 --> 00:12:16.970
but they want to also build a port,

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:22.560
a big port that some people
suggest has dual use,

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:25.340
meaning civilian and military and

00:12:29.070 --> 00:12:30.490
Myanmar's held them off.

00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:34.280
And finally, they have
renegotiated repeatedly

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:39.130
and scaled it back and
still they have not,

00:12:39.130 --> 00:12:40.707
no work has started on it.

00:12:40.707 --> 00:12:45.430
And the same is true with the Myanmar,

00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:48.500
the China Myanmar economic corridor,

00:12:48.500 --> 00:12:50.500
which is to lead from Puning down

00:12:51.774 --> 00:12:55.720
to several major cities in Myanmar

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:59.850
and include roads and railroads.

00:12:59.850 --> 00:13:03.600
And Xi Jinping, the Chinese leader,

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:05.110
was there two years ago

00:13:05.110 --> 00:13:08.140
and really put a lot of
pressure on them to get going.

00:13:08.140 --> 00:13:09.030
Nothing happened.

00:13:09.030 --> 00:13:11.210
They had elections a few weeks ago,

00:13:11.210 --> 00:13:16.210
and Xi just called Xi
again this week and said,

00:13:16.217 --> 00:13:17.880
"You got to get cranking."

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:19.350
But Myanmar really

00:13:19.350 --> 00:13:24.110
is almost as nationalistic
as the Vietnamese.

00:13:24.110 --> 00:13:26.654
And so it is tough

00:13:26.654 --> 00:13:30.800
for China to get much going there as well.

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:33.220
We don't know exactly how much

00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:37.489
China has invested in the Belt
and Road in Southeast Asia,

00:13:37.489 --> 00:13:40.530
but there's a consulting
company in Washington

00:13:40.530 --> 00:13:43.510
called RWR Associates

00:13:43.510 --> 00:13:46.590
and they estimate the
number at about 200 million

00:13:46.590 --> 00:13:49.540
in the five years after 2013.

00:13:49.540 --> 00:13:51.080
But having visited the countries

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:54.350
and visited a lot of
projects in the two years,

00:13:54.350 --> 00:13:57.736
2018 and '19, that I was writing the book,

00:13:57.736 --> 00:14:00.433
I really think that number is pretty high.

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:03.890
Some projects also get bogged down in,

00:14:03.890 --> 00:14:05.500
sorry, I'm spending a lot of time on this,

00:14:05.500 --> 00:14:09.373
but this is really a
signature Chinese project.

00:14:10.253 --> 00:14:11.086
And it's good to know

00:14:11.086 --> 00:14:14.730
that despite the coverage
you get in the Chinese media,

00:14:14.730 --> 00:14:18.083
that this thing is not
cranking along by any means.

00:14:20.085 --> 00:14:24.787
The previous prime minister
of Malaysia, Najib Razak,

00:14:26.437 --> 00:14:29.830
signed an agreement to build
these East Coast Rail Link,

00:14:29.830 --> 00:14:34.007
but he was defeated in an
election in May of 2018

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:39.800
and his successor Mahathir Mohamad 2.0,

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:44.260
he came back after once running
the country for 20 years

00:14:44.260 --> 00:14:45.680
and then leaving for awhile,

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:50.670
he was reelected and he put
a stop to it just because of,

00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:52.930
he thought China had overcharged.

00:14:52.930 --> 00:14:56.200
He thought that there
was a lot of kickbacks

00:14:56.200 --> 00:14:58.917
going to Najib and to Najib's

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:03.000
very distressed state investment company

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:06.273
known as 1Malaysia
Development Berhad or 1MDB.

00:15:10.510 --> 00:15:15.510
And eventually China agreed to negotiate

00:15:15.550 --> 00:15:20.550
but the trial, Najib is still under trial

00:15:22.280 --> 00:15:23.980
for some of the corruption

00:15:23.980 --> 00:15:27.220
that was spinoff of this and other,

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:29.920
this project and others,

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:33.100
that China was involved in
or were domestic projects.

00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:35.330
And we could go on in that vein,

00:15:35.330 --> 00:15:39.507
but I'm gonna get going
here on some other themes.

00:15:42.316 --> 00:15:44.160
I should say, just one more thing.

00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:45.750
If you push back,

00:15:45.750 --> 00:15:48.630
as the Laos proved, as Malaysia proved,

00:15:48.630 --> 00:15:51.900
if you push back against
projects, even once they're going,

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:55.530
you can get China to renegotiate.

00:15:55.530 --> 00:15:58.090
They have been somewhat reasonable

00:15:58.090 --> 00:16:01.383
in showing flexibility and
willingness to compromise.

00:16:02.511 --> 00:16:04.336
For some countries,

00:16:04.336 --> 00:16:07.710
cooperating too much with China

00:16:07.710 --> 00:16:12.710
is very controversial domestically.

00:16:12.717 --> 00:16:17.480
Last year in 2019, Joko Widodo or Joko Wi,

00:16:17.480 --> 00:16:19.010
as he's known for short,

00:16:19.010 --> 00:16:22.690
was running again for
president of Indonesia.

00:16:22.690 --> 00:16:26.559
And he has relied a lot on China
for infrastructure projects

00:16:26.559 --> 00:16:28.800
beyond the one that I mentioned,

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:31.740
the high speed rail
from Jakarta to Bandung.

00:16:31.740 --> 00:16:36.740
And he got a lot of criticisms
from his challengers

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:39.760
for depending too much on China.

00:16:39.760 --> 00:16:43.170
And I may, I'll get back to Indonesia.

00:16:43.170 --> 00:16:48.170
Indonesia has a unique set
of attitudes toward China

00:16:49.435 --> 00:16:51.970
dating back to 1965,

00:16:51.970 --> 00:16:54.660
when China has been
accused of being involved

00:16:56.010 --> 00:16:58.263
in supporting a coup against Sukarno,

00:16:59.437 --> 00:17:00.360
who was then the president.

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:04.057
The other area in which
China is very involved,

00:17:04.057 --> 00:17:06.590
and this is probably much more successful,

00:17:06.590 --> 00:17:09.810
is in the so-called digital silk road.

00:17:09.810 --> 00:17:12.779
So that's the digital economy.

00:17:12.779 --> 00:17:16.360
China is very active in
investing in unicorns,

00:17:16.360 --> 00:17:20.150
the startups worth over a billion dollars,

00:17:20.150 --> 00:17:23.820
E-commerce, various E-payment schemes.

00:17:23.820 --> 00:17:27.580
Huawei in Southeast Asia
is very active in 5G.

00:17:27.580 --> 00:17:32.134
You may remember that president Trump

00:17:32.134 --> 00:17:37.020
has been very much opposed

00:17:37.020 --> 00:17:40.287
to Huawei being involved in 5G in the US

00:17:40.287 --> 00:17:44.550
and has got many friends and allies

00:17:44.550 --> 00:17:47.710
in Europe and Australia and New Zealand

00:17:49.590 --> 00:17:54.590
to shun Huawei out of fear that they will,

00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:56.780
that their technology'll be used

00:17:56.780 --> 00:18:00.003
to spy on the local population.

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:03.510
But these companies like Huawei

00:18:03.510 --> 00:18:06.450
are also involved in
artificial intelligence,

00:18:06.450 --> 00:18:07.760
facial recognition.

00:18:07.760 --> 00:18:10.813
In the Singapore airport,
you have facial recognition.

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:16.570
That's been produced by I think it is way.

00:18:16.570 --> 00:18:20.360
But Chinese firms like
Alibaba, Tencent, JD.com,

00:18:20.360 --> 00:18:25.360
are also investing billions
in startups, like GoJet,

00:18:27.038 --> 00:18:32.038
the motorcycle Uber
kind of thing, company.

00:18:32.250 --> 00:18:33.510
It's broader than that

00:18:33.510 --> 00:18:35.350
but that's how it sort of started,

00:18:35.350 --> 00:18:40.350
Tokopedia, Traveloka and
various China fin tech,

00:18:40.390 --> 00:18:42.300
Chinese fin tech companies

00:18:42.300 --> 00:18:44.150
are also very active in Indonesia.

00:18:44.150 --> 00:18:47.050
About two-thirds of China's investment

00:18:47.050 --> 00:18:50.940
in the e-commerce and the digital spaces

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:53.430
in Indonesia and Singapore.

00:18:53.430 --> 00:18:54.700
I guess that makes sense.

00:18:54.700 --> 00:18:56.360
Indonesia is the biggest country

00:18:56.360 --> 00:18:58.487
with 160, 170 million people

00:18:58.487 --> 00:19:02.420
and Singapore is by far the wealthiest.

00:19:02.420 --> 00:19:05.020
There's only two countries
that are shunning Huawei

00:19:05.020 --> 00:19:07.658
and that is, not surprisingly,

00:19:07.658 --> 00:19:10.340
Erik, Vietnam, as you probably know,

00:19:10.340 --> 00:19:12.580
is not terribly enthusiastic

00:19:12.580 --> 00:19:15.330
and has shunned Huawei,

00:19:15.330 --> 00:19:18.570
and Singapore for 5G

00:19:18.570 --> 00:19:23.570
is giving the biggest contracts
to Ericsson and Nokia,

00:19:23.620 --> 00:19:26.553
but it's giving Huawei
some minor roles, yeah.

00:19:30.777 --> 00:19:32.310
The ethnic Chinese,

00:19:32.310 --> 00:19:34.780
there are, we don't know exactly how many,

00:19:34.780 --> 00:19:36.420
but probably about two percent

00:19:36.420 --> 00:19:41.420
of Southeast Asia's
population is ethnic Chinese.

00:19:41.680 --> 00:19:44.905
Many of them came over the centuries,

00:19:44.905 --> 00:19:47.610
including at the end of the Xing dynasty.

00:19:47.610 --> 00:19:50.110
They came to Southeast Asia off,

00:19:50.110 --> 00:19:54.320
mostly in search of
economic opportunities.

00:19:54.320 --> 00:19:56.300
They came to places like Malaysia

00:19:56.300 --> 00:19:58.170
to aid the British colonial effort,

00:19:58.170 --> 00:20:03.030
help with rubber and palm and tin oil,

00:20:03.030 --> 00:20:07.800
tin oil, tin mining operations.

00:20:08.670 --> 00:20:13.390
But then they started
to play over the years,

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:15.950
a much greater role in the economy.

00:20:15.950 --> 00:20:19.890
So they play quite a big role.

00:20:19.890 --> 00:20:23.420
The thing that really stunned me though

00:20:23.420 --> 00:20:25.910
was the new migration,

00:20:25.910 --> 00:20:29.000
which I don't think Beijing
has anything to do with,

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:31.570
but you're seeing increasing numbers

00:20:31.570 --> 00:20:35.080
of Chinese come into Southeast Asia.

00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:36.960
The places where you see the most of them

00:20:36.960 --> 00:20:40.560
are in in Northern
Myanmar, north of Mandalay,

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:42.020
and Northern Laos,

00:20:42.020 --> 00:20:46.202
where they are involved in plantations,

00:20:46.202 --> 00:20:51.202
rubber, bananas, watermelons,
green beans, pumpkins,

00:20:52.920 --> 00:20:56.185
just an awful lot of agricultural,

00:20:56.185 --> 00:20:58.853
industrial agricultural products.

00:21:00.040 --> 00:21:02.350
Creating a lot of tensions in the region

00:21:02.350 --> 00:21:03.690
because they use a lot

00:21:03.690 --> 00:21:07.480
of chemical pesticides
and chemical fertilizer,

00:21:07.480 --> 00:21:12.030
which is polluting the rivers and causing,

00:21:12.030 --> 00:21:16.707
causing bereft animals to die.

00:21:21.825 --> 00:21:24.400
Then they also move into some cities.

00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:26.130
And there are quite a few,

00:21:26.130 --> 00:21:31.130
obviously there were a lot
in Sihanoukville in Cambodia,

00:21:31.420 --> 00:21:34.670
especially until the end of last year,

00:21:34.670 --> 00:21:39.670
when China got Hun Sen, the
prime minister of Cambodia,

00:21:40.330 --> 00:21:45.330
to agree to end online gaming
and a lot of casino gambling.

00:21:47.210 --> 00:21:49.690
But there's also a fair,

00:21:49.690 --> 00:21:53.790
quite a large number of ethnic
Chinese moving to Manila.

00:21:53.790 --> 00:21:58.690
And then the guys who left Sihanoukville

00:21:58.690 --> 00:22:01.110
after the clamp down on gambling,

00:22:01.110 --> 00:22:06.060
they moved to Kayin or
Kayin state in Myanmar

00:22:06.060 --> 00:22:09.655
and they set up casinos there,

00:22:09.655 --> 00:22:12.303
Sihanoukoko being one
of the more famous ones.

00:22:14.387 --> 00:22:18.510
So I guess I'm a little bit
concerned that long term

00:22:18.510 --> 00:22:23.510
the ethnic population in Northern
Myanmar and Northern Laos

00:22:25.210 --> 00:22:28.770
can have a huge cultural
and political impact

00:22:29.768 --> 00:22:34.570
in those parts of those two
countries in particular.

00:22:36.474 --> 00:22:39.660
The history of China with Southeast Asia

00:22:39.660 --> 00:22:42.453
obviously goes back to for millennia.

00:22:43.762 --> 00:22:48.543
China once controlled northern Vietnam

00:22:48.543 --> 00:22:53.293
as a colonial power
for almost 1,000 years.

00:22:55.562 --> 00:22:59.577
But after the communists
took over in 1949,

00:22:59.577 --> 00:23:04.080
the communist party
supported communist forces

00:23:04.080 --> 00:23:06.350
against most of the governments

00:23:06.350 --> 00:23:09.560
that were all establishing themselves

00:23:09.560 --> 00:23:11.300
after the end of World War II

00:23:11.300 --> 00:23:13.800
when many of them got independence

00:23:13.800 --> 00:23:16.633
from their European colonial masters.

00:23:17.500 --> 00:23:19.397
And this period

00:23:19.397 --> 00:23:24.397
when China was supporting
domestic communists,

00:23:25.510 --> 00:23:29.473
it still remains a legacy in
many of these countries today.

00:23:30.386 --> 00:23:31.219
So this is what,

00:23:31.219 --> 00:23:33.230
what is one of the challenges

00:23:33.230 --> 00:23:37.080
for Jokowi and other leaders in Indonesia,

00:23:37.080 --> 00:23:39.350
that many people still are convinced

00:23:39.350 --> 00:23:41.970
that China was involved in a coup

00:23:41.970 --> 00:23:44.660
in which hundreds of thousands of people

00:23:44.660 --> 00:23:46.753
in the end got killed.

00:23:47.729 --> 00:23:51.237
And there's a lot of fear that China

00:23:52.890 --> 00:23:55.373
may still be involved politically.

00:23:57.620 --> 00:24:00.110
Jokowi himself, the first
time he ran for power,

00:24:00.110 --> 00:24:03.000
he was a mayor of Solo,

00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:07.623
a little town in Java.

00:24:08.628 --> 00:24:10.830
He was accused of being both a Christian

00:24:10.830 --> 00:24:12.110
and an ethnic Chinese

00:24:12.110 --> 00:24:17.110
and he's pretty clearly
an Indonesian Javanese

00:24:19.990 --> 00:24:21.720
who's a Muslim.

00:24:21.720 --> 00:24:26.720
But charging people with being Chinese

00:24:27.310 --> 00:24:31.480
is often a weapon you
can use against them.

00:24:31.480 --> 00:24:32.520
And that's what happened

00:24:32.520 --> 00:24:36.760
to mayor who replaced Jokowi in Jakarta.

00:24:36.760 --> 00:24:40.130
He was ethnic Chinese.

00:24:40.130 --> 00:24:41.463
His nickname was Ahawk.

00:24:42.313 --> 00:24:45.300
He was ethnic Chinese and a Christian,

00:24:45.300 --> 00:24:48.690
and he got nailed with allegations

00:24:48.690 --> 00:24:53.690
of blasphemy for when he tried
to tell a joke about Muslims

00:24:56.500 --> 00:24:58.993
and it did not cover very well at all.

00:25:01.030 --> 00:25:05.350
Opinion polls, they're tough to figure out

00:25:05.350 --> 00:25:08.730
how popular China is but

00:25:10.837 --> 00:25:15.340
the Institute for Southeast
Asian Studies in Singapore

00:25:16.280 --> 00:25:18.220
does annual surveys.

00:25:18.220 --> 00:25:21.450
And the survey that came out in January,

00:25:21.450 --> 00:25:22.870
these are elite polls

00:25:22.870 --> 00:25:27.870
where they ask officials and academics

00:25:28.130 --> 00:25:31.110
and business people and
journalists and NGO people

00:25:33.101 --> 00:25:35.600
to give their views.

00:25:35.600 --> 00:25:38.410
And the poll that came out in January

00:25:38.410 --> 00:25:42.240
had 79% of Southeast Asians

00:25:42.240 --> 00:25:46.550
saying China had the most
economic influence in the region.

00:25:46.550 --> 00:25:49.470
About only eight percent

00:25:49.470 --> 00:25:51.450
thought that about the United States.

00:25:51.450 --> 00:25:54.180
But even more surprising was that 52%

00:25:54.180 --> 00:25:56.460
said China was the most politically

00:25:56.460 --> 00:26:00.320
and strategically
influential in Southeast Asia

00:26:00.320 --> 00:26:02.790
and only 27%, about half,

00:26:02.790 --> 00:26:04.713
thought that about the United States.

00:26:05.660 --> 00:26:10.660
But if they were forced to
choose between China and the US,

00:26:11.130 --> 00:26:13.210
which of course Southeast
Asia doesn't want to do,

00:26:13.210 --> 00:26:18.210
but 46% chose China, 54%
chose the United States,

00:26:18.622 --> 00:26:22.090
which is pretty close actually.

00:26:22.090 --> 00:26:25.780
China has really stepped up
military to military ties

00:26:25.780 --> 00:26:28.160
in recent years.

00:26:28.160 --> 00:26:31.130
It's sold some arms to some countries,

00:26:31.130 --> 00:26:33.940
particularly Thailand.

00:26:33.940 --> 00:26:36.470
Since the coup had purchased,

00:26:36.470 --> 00:26:39.999
the 2014 coop purchased some tanks,

00:26:39.999 --> 00:26:42.637
it ordered some submarines.

00:26:42.637 --> 00:26:46.710
Malaysia has had some
coastal patrol vessels

00:26:46.710 --> 00:26:50.413
purchased from China.

00:26:51.500 --> 00:26:55.140
Myanmar and Cambodia also get

00:26:55.140 --> 00:26:57.823
quite a bit of military
hardware from China.

00:26:59.630 --> 00:27:01.637
Some countries like Indonesia

00:27:01.637 --> 00:27:04.680
occasionally look to buy more,

00:27:04.680 --> 00:27:08.670
but really in the end, they haven't.

00:27:08.670 --> 00:27:12.760
China is also stepping up
its military exercises.

00:27:12.760 --> 00:27:15.180
Still way behind where the US is

00:27:15.180 --> 00:27:17.943
in terms of frequency and size.

00:27:19.040 --> 00:27:22.000
They also offered to do some training

00:27:22.894 --> 00:27:27.730
of Southeast Asian officers
in Chinese military schools,

00:27:27.730 --> 00:27:31.363
but it's still way
behind what the US does.

00:27:32.910 --> 00:27:37.910
China is really very big in soft power.

00:27:39.367 --> 00:27:43.950
It provides just oodles of scholarships.

00:27:43.950 --> 00:27:44.820
And I say oodles,

00:27:44.820 --> 00:27:47.647
because we it's really
hard to get numbers,

00:27:47.647 --> 00:27:50.950
total numbers for Southeast Asia.

00:27:50.950 --> 00:27:55.360
But in 2016, there were almost 70,000

00:27:55.360 --> 00:27:58.390
Southeast Asians studying in China.

00:27:58.390 --> 00:28:01.140
By far the biggest number was the Thai's.

00:28:01.140 --> 00:28:05.103
23,000 followed by Indonesians, 15,000.

00:28:07.530 --> 00:28:10.560
Vietnam I think was 11 or 12,000,

00:28:10.560 --> 00:28:12.703
which is still pretty high.

00:28:12.703 --> 00:28:14.710
China has also been very busy

00:28:14.710 --> 00:28:16.620
setting up Confucius institutes,

00:28:16.620 --> 00:28:21.490
which provide Mandarin language training.

00:28:21.490 --> 00:28:23.500
There's about 30 now in the region.

00:28:23.500 --> 00:28:26.783
Half of them, 15 are in
Thailand, five in Malaysia,

00:28:29.746 --> 00:28:33.108
two in the Philippines, one in Vietnam.

00:28:33.108 --> 00:28:36.770
And they're also inviting many more people

00:28:36.770 --> 00:28:39.420
from Southeast Asia to come visit.

00:28:39.420 --> 00:28:42.000
This includes party officials,

00:28:42.000 --> 00:28:43.760
not only from communist countries,

00:28:43.760 --> 00:28:46.050
but also from Malaysia and Indonesia.

00:28:46.050 --> 00:28:47.833
They invite party officials,

00:28:49.125 --> 00:28:51.763
PDIP, which is Jokowi's party.

00:28:55.018 --> 00:28:59.060
His party sends officials for training

00:28:59.060 --> 00:29:01.750
in terms of how do you do budgets,

00:29:01.750 --> 00:29:03.950
how you recruit new
members, that kind of stuff.

00:29:03.950 --> 00:29:06.713
It's not Marxist, Leninist training.

00:29:11.500 --> 00:29:13.600
President Duterte in the Philippines, his,

00:29:14.520 --> 00:29:16.868
what's it, PL, oh boy,

00:29:16.868 --> 00:29:19.200
I'm forgetting the name of his
party right now, the acronym,

00:29:19.200 --> 00:29:22.970
but his party also sends people to,

00:29:22.970 --> 00:29:25.853
party leaders to China.

00:29:26.769 --> 00:29:28.410
Lots of journalists,

00:29:28.410 --> 00:29:30.080
lots of religious leaders.

00:29:30.080 --> 00:29:31.270
They really are,

00:29:31.270 --> 00:29:36.270
China since the treatment
of the Uighurs in Xinjiang

00:29:37.640 --> 00:29:40.690
has become more high profile globally,

00:29:40.690 --> 00:29:43.003
has invited a lot of Muslims,

00:29:43.003 --> 00:29:46.153
Muslim leaders, Imams to visit Xinjiang.

00:29:49.261 --> 00:29:52.809
And many of them come back

00:29:52.809 --> 00:29:53.642
speaking very positively.

00:29:55.041 --> 00:29:56.960
It's like a work camp atmosphere

00:29:56.960 --> 00:29:59.840
where they can pray five times a day,

00:29:59.840 --> 00:30:02.063
they can need Halal food, et cetera.

00:30:03.320 --> 00:30:04.620
Some are so positive

00:30:04.620 --> 00:30:06.650
that the Chinese ambassador will visit,

00:30:06.650 --> 00:30:08.560
as happened in the case of one village

00:30:08.560 --> 00:30:11.640
in Java in Indonesia.

00:30:11.640 --> 00:30:16.550
And he offered to build
them a new irrigation system

00:30:16.550 --> 00:30:21.550
thanks to the Imam's very
positive effusive description

00:30:21.800 --> 00:30:23.563
of how the Uighur's are treated.

00:30:24.631 --> 00:30:28.050
China, I really worked hard

00:30:28.050 --> 00:30:29.230
at trying to figure out

00:30:29.230 --> 00:30:31.993
the level of Chinese political influence.

00:30:33.290 --> 00:30:35.523
You do see some examples,

00:30:35.523 --> 00:30:38.140
but it's not nearly as obvious

00:30:38.140 --> 00:30:41.229
as it is in Australia or New Zealand.

00:30:41.229 --> 00:30:44.650
In the 2018 elections in Malaysia,

00:30:44.650 --> 00:30:46.240
the Chinese ambassador,

00:30:46.240 --> 00:30:47.900
he actually went and campaigned

00:30:47.900 --> 00:30:50.820
for some of the ruling party officials,

00:30:50.820 --> 00:30:51.908
(speaking in foreign language)

00:30:51.908 --> 00:30:56.310
and the Malaysia Chinese
Association officials.

00:30:56.310 --> 00:30:59.043
They actually did quite
poorly and lost the election.

00:31:01.477 --> 00:31:05.820
From about the mid 2016 to mid 2017,

00:31:05.820 --> 00:31:08.853
China and Singapore were at,

00:31:08.853 --> 00:31:10.130
China was in the middle

00:31:10.130 --> 00:31:14.148
of an let's isolate Singapore campaign,

00:31:14.148 --> 00:31:18.410
partially because the prime
minister had been critic,

00:31:18.410 --> 00:31:21.520
had urged China to follow the rule of law

00:31:21.520 --> 00:31:26.501
in response to the UN arbitral tribunal

00:31:26.501 --> 00:31:28.530
that the Philippines had brought

00:31:28.530 --> 00:31:33.083
to ruling overwhelmingly in
favor of the Philippines.

00:31:36.870 --> 00:31:39.903
China went ballistic and started,

00:31:40.950 --> 00:31:44.970
Singapore is the biggest investor in China

00:31:44.970 --> 00:31:47.110
and really started putting pressure,

00:31:47.110 --> 00:31:49.070
China put pressure on businessmen.

00:31:49.070 --> 00:31:51.784
If you want to keep operating here,

00:31:51.784 --> 00:31:56.120
you will have to get your
government to change policy,

00:31:56.120 --> 00:31:57.870
which really, should I say,

00:31:57.870 --> 00:32:00.580
really irritated the Singapore government

00:32:00.580 --> 00:32:02.560
and they didn't really respond

00:32:02.560 --> 00:32:04.823
to the businessmen's lobby, I guess.

00:32:06.290 --> 00:32:07.940
China is also very active

00:32:07.940 --> 00:32:10.430
in phishing and hacking.

00:32:10.430 --> 00:32:11.740
And Vietnam,

00:32:11.740 --> 00:32:16.170
despite the fact that it
might be a communist ally,

00:32:17.520 --> 00:32:19.336
technically quote, unquote,

00:32:19.336 --> 00:32:23.414
is often the brunt of it.

00:32:23.414 --> 00:32:26.720
When Vietnam was gonna host the APEC,

00:32:26.720 --> 00:32:31.193
the Asia Pacific Economic
Cooperation Forum in 2017,

00:32:31.193 --> 00:32:34.423
that was gonna be attended
by both Xi and Trump,

00:32:35.571 --> 00:32:39.403
China hacked pretty badly.

00:32:39.403 --> 00:32:42.020
They often use phishing techniques

00:32:42.020 --> 00:32:45.170
where they get officials

00:32:45.170 --> 00:32:48.453
to open their open certain software,

00:32:49.978 --> 00:32:52.380
malware that goes into their computers,

00:32:52.380 --> 00:32:57.380
and they give a dump of
their negotiating strategy.

00:32:57.980 --> 00:33:00.040
The Chinese also regularly look

00:33:00.040 --> 00:33:03.400
for Vietnam's strategy
in the South China Sea,

00:33:03.400 --> 00:33:07.470
try to figure out what the
party is saying about that.

00:33:07.470 --> 00:33:11.810
And then ahead of the
completion of the negotiations

00:33:11.810 --> 00:33:14.330
for the regional comprehensive
economic partnership,

00:33:14.330 --> 00:33:17.803
which was just signed what
about two or three weeks ago,

00:33:19.398 --> 00:33:24.263
the Chinese also hacked into
Vietnam's system quite a bit.

00:33:25.200 --> 00:33:29.740
Indonesian officials in
the elections in 2019

00:33:29.740 --> 00:33:31.720
charged both the Chinese and Russians

00:33:31.720 --> 00:33:33.833
with hacking into their voter lists.

00:33:35.902 --> 00:33:37.880
The Chinese hacked into SingHealth

00:33:39.476 --> 00:33:42.870
where they downloaded just
reams of documentation

00:33:42.870 --> 00:33:46.670
about the health conditions
of various Singaporeans,

00:33:46.670 --> 00:33:48.463
including Prime Minister Lee.

00:33:50.620 --> 00:33:51.870
Some of that stuff

00:33:51.870 --> 00:33:55.010
irritates the Southeast Asians
quite a bit, but they say,

00:33:55.010 --> 00:33:58.118
they point out that China
is not the only big power

00:33:58.118 --> 00:34:00.273
that's constantly spying on them,

00:34:01.570 --> 00:34:03.630
which I won't name that other big power,

00:34:03.630 --> 00:34:06.360
you probably know where you're sitting.

00:34:06.360 --> 00:34:11.160
And I'll conclude with
some comments on water.

00:34:11.160 --> 00:34:14.060
I actually realized I'm going
a lot longer than I thought.

00:34:15.220 --> 00:34:17.700
One of the concerns is what's happening

00:34:21.178 --> 00:34:22.011
on the Mekong.

00:34:22.011 --> 00:34:26.410
China has built 11 dams in the
upper reaches of the Mekong,

00:34:26.410 --> 00:34:29.840
and then has helped build
dams along the Mekong

00:34:29.840 --> 00:34:34.150
as we come through Laos,
Thailand, Cambodia,

00:34:34.150 --> 00:34:36.463
and into Vietnam.

00:34:39.885 --> 00:34:44.885
The water from China contributes
about 25 or so percent

00:34:45.750 --> 00:34:48.229
of the water in the Mekong.

00:34:48.229 --> 00:34:52.350
And the Mekong is about,

00:34:52.350 --> 00:34:56.310
in Southeast Asia, in Laos,
Thailand, Myanmar, and Vietnam,

00:34:56.310 --> 00:34:57.790
about 60 million people

00:34:57.790 --> 00:35:00.563
make their living along
that fishing and farming.

00:35:02.220 --> 00:35:07.220
The Mekong is one of
the most fertile fishing

00:35:08.640 --> 00:35:10.860
sources in the world

00:35:10.860 --> 00:35:15.860
and one of the biggest spawning areas

00:35:16.400 --> 00:35:20.390
for fish along the
Mekong is the Tonle Sap,

00:35:20.390 --> 00:35:23.620
which is the lake in
the middle of Cambodia

00:35:23.620 --> 00:35:26.020
that during rainy season explodes

00:35:26.020 --> 00:35:29.014
as a tributary of the Mekong

00:35:29.014 --> 00:35:32.250
carries water up into the Tonle Sap

00:35:32.250 --> 00:35:34.810
and brings a lot of fish with it.

00:35:34.810 --> 00:35:36.820
And then in dry season,

00:35:36.820 --> 00:35:38.980
the water flows back down

00:35:38.980 --> 00:35:43.980
and moves the fish back into
the Mekong main tributary.

00:35:44.010 --> 00:35:48.450
And in Vietnam's Mekong Delta,

00:35:48.450 --> 00:35:52.740
the level of water matters
a lot because it is,

00:35:52.740 --> 00:35:57.350
the Mekong Delta is the
bread basket of Vietnam.

00:35:57.350 --> 00:36:00.570
It is also below sea level.

00:36:00.570 --> 00:36:02.870
So if it doesn't get water from the Mekong

00:36:02.870 --> 00:36:05.145
and silt all the way from Tibet,

00:36:05.145 --> 00:36:07.390
it can slip into the sea.

00:36:07.390 --> 00:36:09.340
And so what's happened,

00:36:09.340 --> 00:36:12.853
the last two years as
China has opened up it's,

00:36:14.520 --> 00:36:18.130
or really started running its 11 dams,

00:36:18.130 --> 00:36:22.085
the dam number seven and dam number 11,

00:36:22.085 --> 00:36:26.993
those two hold enough water
to fill the Chesapeake Bay,

00:36:28.564 --> 00:36:29.830
which is not terribly far

00:36:29.830 --> 00:36:32.590
from where you guys or
I am sitting right now.

00:36:32.590 --> 00:36:37.070
And this has caused a lot of,

00:36:37.070 --> 00:36:39.140
because of water being,

00:36:39.140 --> 00:36:44.140
rain being less the last few
years than it had been earlier

00:36:44.982 --> 00:36:47.170
has caused a severe drought.

00:36:47.170 --> 00:36:49.180
And so we are,

00:36:49.180 --> 00:36:52.553
the agricultural crops are being damaged.

00:36:54.476 --> 00:36:58.800
The spawning of fish is being hurt

00:36:58.800 --> 00:37:02.223
and the Mekong Delta in
Vietnam is being hurt.

00:37:05.785 --> 00:37:07.970
So this has become a huge issue.

00:37:07.970 --> 00:37:11.770
And the Mekong countries

00:37:11.770 --> 00:37:14.620
have started to press China on this.

00:37:14.620 --> 00:37:18.411
But so far, China has only said,

00:37:18.411 --> 00:37:19.670
first of all, they started

00:37:19.670 --> 00:37:22.020
by saying they also faced the drought.

00:37:22.020 --> 00:37:24.270
We now know that they didn't in fact,

00:37:24.270 --> 00:37:27.340
because a group called "Eyes on the Earth"

00:37:27.340 --> 00:37:30.130
took satellite photos early this year,

00:37:30.130 --> 00:37:32.430
earlier this year about
six or eight months ago,

00:37:32.430 --> 00:37:35.810
and found the massive quantities of water

00:37:35.810 --> 00:37:37.360
that I was referring to.

00:37:37.360 --> 00:37:41.430
And so they, the ASEAN's started

00:37:42.750 --> 00:37:44.920
putting a lot of pressure on China.

00:37:44.920 --> 00:37:47.930
China has agreed to start giving them data

00:37:47.930 --> 00:37:49.853
on how much water they're holding.

00:37:50.699 --> 00:37:52.250
We'll have to see

00:37:53.127 --> 00:37:58.127
if the countries of Laos,
Thailand, Cambodia, and Vietnam

00:37:58.620 --> 00:38:00.110
can exert more pressure

00:38:00.110 --> 00:38:04.800
so that China will be more accommodating.

00:38:04.800 --> 00:38:05.710
I don't know

00:38:08.010 --> 00:38:09.140
if I should,

00:38:09.140 --> 00:38:12.300
Erik, should I talk a little
bit about the South China Sea,

00:38:12.300 --> 00:38:13.750
or do you think most people here

00:38:13.750 --> 00:38:15.210
know about the South China Sea

00:38:15.210 --> 00:38:17.140
and we can leave it for
question and answer?

00:38:17.140 --> 00:38:20.303
'Cause I realize we have
about 20 minutes left.

00:38:22.290 --> 00:38:24.960
- I'd say that if you
just mention it briefly

00:38:24.960 --> 00:38:26.450
and then wrap it to a close,

00:38:26.450 --> 00:38:27.283
then we'll have time.

00:38:27.283 --> 00:38:29.330
And we can linger a
little bit beyond the hour

00:38:29.330 --> 00:38:31.878
if you have time, but that's up to you.

00:38:31.878 --> 00:38:33.950
Some people will leave right at the hour,

00:38:33.950 --> 00:38:36.430
but I'm happy stick around too.

00:38:36.430 --> 00:38:37.263
- Okay.

00:38:37.263 --> 00:38:41.560
So I'll try to go over the
South China Sea very quickly.

00:38:41.560 --> 00:38:44.330
I think that is something that
is covered more in the news

00:38:44.330 --> 00:38:46.464
and the US government talks about it.

00:38:46.464 --> 00:38:49.020
But the South China Sea,

00:38:49.020 --> 00:38:50.880
and this is maybe, you asked me Erik

00:38:50.880 --> 00:38:51.860
if I was gonna use a map,

00:38:51.860 --> 00:38:55.080
maybe it would have been
useful to have a map of this,

00:38:55.080 --> 00:38:59.510
but the South China Sea
has a big U-shaped line,

00:38:59.510 --> 00:39:02.440
a dotted line in it, nine dotted line,

00:39:02.440 --> 00:39:07.090
which is China's claims to about
80% of the South China Sea.

00:39:07.090 --> 00:39:10.240
It claims the fish,

00:39:10.240 --> 00:39:14.553
the seafood and the
hydrocarbons in that area,

00:39:15.721 --> 00:39:20.721
which is also where Vietnam,
Philippines, and Malaysia

00:39:21.570 --> 00:39:23.420
and to some extent, Indonesia,

00:39:23.420 --> 00:39:24.960
which actually isn't a claimant,

00:39:24.960 --> 00:39:27.480
the claimant countries
disputing with China

00:39:27.480 --> 00:39:30.760
are Vietnam, Malaysia,
Philippines, and Brunei.

00:39:30.760 --> 00:39:35.176
But part of the nine dash line
runs through Northern Natuna,

00:39:35.176 --> 00:39:38.693
which is in the far
Northwestern part of Indonesia.

00:39:42.904 --> 00:39:47.670
China's interest in the South China Sea

00:39:47.670 --> 00:39:49.380
is relatively recent.

00:39:49.380 --> 00:39:54.380
In 2009, based on the UN
conventional law of sea

00:39:54.480 --> 00:39:57.363
that was a deadline for
countries to put in their claims.

00:39:58.207 --> 00:40:01.750
The main Southeast Asian
countries put in their claims.

00:40:01.750 --> 00:40:06.170
China was really irritated
at how much of their claims

00:40:06.170 --> 00:40:09.160
overlapped with those of China.

00:40:09.160 --> 00:40:14.160
In 2012, China took Scarborough
Shoal from the Philippines

00:40:18.056 --> 00:40:21.080
in a pretty dramatic move.

00:40:21.080 --> 00:40:22.670
The Philippines brought the case

00:40:22.670 --> 00:40:24.900
that I referred to earlier in 2013.

00:40:27.622 --> 00:40:32.622
In 2016, the arbitral
tribunal ruled against China.

00:40:32.690 --> 00:40:37.250
2016 or 2015, boy, both
dates stick in my head now,

00:40:37.250 --> 00:40:42.250
but ruled against China
and China went ballistic,

00:40:44.410 --> 00:40:46.300
ignored the ruling totally.

00:40:46.300 --> 00:40:47.660
It had earlier,

00:40:47.660 --> 00:40:49.980
as soon as the Philippines
brought the case,

00:40:49.980 --> 00:40:52.424
started building artificial islands

00:40:52.424 --> 00:40:55.313
in the South China Sea,

00:40:57.840 --> 00:40:59.680
built up seven islands

00:40:59.680 --> 00:41:03.520
by dredging sand and grinding up coral,

00:41:03.520 --> 00:41:05.140
which is pretty bad,

00:41:05.140 --> 00:41:08.373
because coral is a natural
habitat for seafood.

00:41:10.315 --> 00:41:15.315
Four of the islands now have
military landing strips,

00:41:15.920 --> 00:41:18.680
so military planes and radar

00:41:18.680 --> 00:41:21.180
and missile installations
have been installed.

00:41:21.180 --> 00:41:24.680
It is now possible for China
to fly from these bases

00:41:24.680 --> 00:41:27.323
to any capital in Southeast Asia.

00:41:28.469 --> 00:41:30.200
Two years ago,

00:41:30.200 --> 00:41:34.360
China really did ramp
up its pressure again

00:41:34.360 --> 00:41:39.360
and tried to press Vietnam and
Malaysia and the Philippines

00:41:40.530 --> 00:41:45.530
to abandon some of their
efforts to look for oil and gas.

00:41:47.240 --> 00:41:50.470
They started harassing Chinese,

00:41:50.470 --> 00:41:52.470
big Chinese coast guard vessels

00:41:52.470 --> 00:41:56.196
and maritime militia vessels,

00:41:56.196 --> 00:41:59.462
began harassing exploration activities,

00:41:59.462 --> 00:42:04.462
and really made it clear,
I think, to Southeast Asia,

00:42:06.998 --> 00:42:09.460
their Southeast Asian competitors,

00:42:09.460 --> 00:42:14.342
that you really should
stop trying to develop

00:42:14.342 --> 00:42:18.280
these hydrocarbons off your coasts

00:42:18.280 --> 00:42:20.650
with foreign companies like Shell

00:42:20.650 --> 00:42:22.680
and Rosneft and Repsol,

00:42:22.680 --> 00:42:25.969
Rosneft being Russian,
Repsol being Spanish.

00:42:25.969 --> 00:42:30.930
And either do it with
us or do it by yourself.

00:42:30.930 --> 00:42:33.130
None of them can do it by themselves.

00:42:33.130 --> 00:42:35.270
So it's really created problems,

00:42:35.270 --> 00:42:37.940
especially for Vietnam
and the Philippines,

00:42:37.940 --> 00:42:41.733
in terms of wanting to
develop gas resources,

00:42:44.604 --> 00:42:48.207
making it possible for
them to fire more hydro,

00:42:49.160 --> 00:42:51.060
fire more power

00:42:51.060 --> 00:42:55.090
using gas rather than dirty
coal burning technology

00:42:55.090 --> 00:42:57.810
that they have all gotten from China.

00:42:57.810 --> 00:43:00.050
And in the case of Malaysia,

00:43:00.050 --> 00:43:04.300
which is the second largest
exporter of oil and gas,

00:43:04.300 --> 00:43:06.990
it is a major source
of government revenue,

00:43:06.990 --> 00:43:10.070
about a quarter of the government's
revenue comes from this.

00:43:10.070 --> 00:43:12.070
And so one doesn't quite know

00:43:12.070 --> 00:43:13.780
how this is all gonna be resolved

00:43:13.780 --> 00:43:18.780
but there is a lot of tension
between these countries.

00:43:20.640 --> 00:43:22.660
The US regularly sales through there

00:43:22.660 --> 00:43:25.793
with so-called freedom
of navigation exercises,

00:43:27.030 --> 00:43:29.240
which is intended to tell the Chinese

00:43:29.240 --> 00:43:30.893
we don't accept your claims.

00:43:32.870 --> 00:43:36.718
Sometimes other countries
have joined the US.

00:43:36.718 --> 00:43:38.340
Earlier this year,

00:43:38.340 --> 00:43:40.790
both Australia and Japan went along

00:43:40.790 --> 00:43:45.790
for a short sale through
by American vessels.

00:43:47.360 --> 00:43:49.300
This is the one area though

00:43:49.300 --> 00:43:54.190
where the US has been relatively quiet.

00:43:54.190 --> 00:43:59.190
But under Trump and beginning in August,

00:43:59.190 --> 00:44:00.990
secretary of state Pompeo

00:44:00.990 --> 00:44:04.160
began very sharply
criticizing the Chinese.

00:44:04.160 --> 00:44:07.040
It was part of a whole anti-China campaign

00:44:07.040 --> 00:44:09.870
that you might know deals with Taiwan,

00:44:09.870 --> 00:44:11.000
it deals with Hong Kong,

00:44:11.000 --> 00:44:13.340
it deals with the Uighur's in Xinjiang

00:44:14.280 --> 00:44:15.690
It deals with Huawei.

00:44:15.690 --> 00:44:20.319
It puts tariffs on various
forms of Chinese trade

00:44:20.319 --> 00:44:22.940
because the US is frustrated

00:44:22.940 --> 00:44:25.870
at Chinese stealing
intellectual property rights

00:44:25.870 --> 00:44:29.730
and forcing US companies
to do technology transfer

00:44:29.730 --> 00:44:31.280
and those kinds of things.

00:44:31.280 --> 00:44:33.830
So the water,

00:44:33.830 --> 00:44:38.120
both the Mekong and the South China Sea

00:44:38.120 --> 00:44:43.120
are two areas where it is
quite possible for accidents

00:44:43.590 --> 00:44:46.830
and that could lead into to more conflict.

00:44:46.830 --> 00:44:50.100
It is the one area in
which China has a bit,

00:44:50.100 --> 00:44:52.200
has a quite a black eye,

00:44:52.200 --> 00:44:56.990
in especially the maritime
Southeast Asian countries.

00:44:56.990 --> 00:44:58.490
So I'm gonna stop there, Erik,

00:44:58.490 --> 00:45:00.810
and let you turn to questions.

00:45:00.810 --> 00:45:03.476
Sorry, that went a little
longer than I intended to.

00:45:03.476 --> 00:45:05.740
- No, Murray, no need to apologize.

00:45:05.740 --> 00:45:07.307
There's a lot to talk about

00:45:07.307 --> 00:45:10.280
and you gave a really
comprehensive coverage

00:45:10.280 --> 00:45:12.730
of so many issues that

00:45:12.730 --> 00:45:14.560
with China's investment

00:45:14.560 --> 00:45:16.750
and interactions with Southeast Asia.

00:45:16.750 --> 00:45:19.090
So let's open it up to questions.

00:45:19.090 --> 00:45:21.749
At this point, anyone who has a question,

00:45:21.749 --> 00:45:26.749
if you can use in the
participants tab of Zoom,

00:45:26.750 --> 00:45:28.954
there is a function to raise your hand.

00:45:28.954 --> 00:45:31.034
That's the easiest way to do it.

00:45:31.034 --> 00:45:33.172
And if you can't find that,

00:45:33.172 --> 00:45:37.190
just send a message to the
chat and I'll note your name.

00:45:37.190 --> 00:45:38.660
We already have two hands up.

00:45:38.660 --> 00:45:42.100
We'll start with Jon
Miceler, I believe it is.

00:45:42.100 --> 00:45:44.868
And after that, will be Greg Miller.

00:45:44.868 --> 00:45:46.020
- [Jon] Thanks very much.

00:45:46.020 --> 00:45:47.200
Can you all hear me?

00:45:47.200 --> 00:45:48.059
- [Erik] Yes, we can.

00:45:48.059 --> 00:45:49.880
- [Jon] Excellent presentation.

00:45:49.880 --> 00:45:50.920
Thank you for your time today.

00:45:50.920 --> 00:45:52.800
I have a quick question

00:45:52.800 --> 00:45:55.050
related to the Belt Road Initiative,

00:45:55.050 --> 00:45:57.140
particularly in Southeast Asia.

00:45:57.140 --> 00:46:02.140
Do you feel that the
conditions could be created

00:46:03.040 --> 00:46:06.426
to make the Belt Road

00:46:06.426 --> 00:46:10.968
an enabling condition for sustainability?

00:46:10.968 --> 00:46:14.431
Building linear infrastructure, railroads,

00:46:14.431 --> 00:46:19.160
roads, high tension wires, you name it,

00:46:19.160 --> 00:46:22.560
I mean, all of that comes
with an ecological cost,

00:46:22.560 --> 00:46:24.630
but there's a lot of science out there

00:46:24.630 --> 00:46:27.750
on how to do that stuff more sustainably.

00:46:27.750 --> 00:46:29.310
And I'm just wondering, in your opinion,

00:46:29.310 --> 00:46:32.930
do you think there is room
for dialogue with China

00:46:32.930 --> 00:46:35.750
to talk about how to
do that in a green way

00:46:35.750 --> 00:46:38.900
that actually makes it a win-win situation

00:46:38.900 --> 00:46:40.970
for all parties involved?

00:46:40.970 --> 00:46:42.960
Or is it really just about

00:46:42.960 --> 00:46:46.620
getting it done the cheapest
way to make money for China

00:46:46.620 --> 00:46:48.750
and hope for the best
for the other countries?

00:46:48.750 --> 00:46:50.400
I mean, do you have any opinion on that?

00:46:50.400 --> 00:46:52.471
'Cause I'd like to think that there was

00:46:52.471 --> 00:46:55.063
some hope and optimism for the former.

00:46:57.513 --> 00:46:58.820
- I think there's hope.

00:46:58.820 --> 00:46:59.990
It'll take some time.

00:46:59.990 --> 00:47:03.350
You may recall that at
the end of last year,

00:47:03.350 --> 00:47:07.390
Xi Jinping hosted a conference

00:47:07.390 --> 00:47:10.980
with main recipients of the Belt and Road

00:47:10.980 --> 00:47:13.210
in Beijing, I think it was in November.

00:47:13.210 --> 00:47:16.960
And he did in his speech acknowledge

00:47:16.960 --> 00:47:21.390
that China needed to be
more green in its projects

00:47:21.390 --> 00:47:24.140
and less corrupt in its projects,

00:47:24.140 --> 00:47:29.053
which I think that was a good start.

00:47:30.150 --> 00:47:31.580
And then you might've seen,

00:47:31.580 --> 00:47:34.110
I'm trying to remember what
the name of the group was.

00:47:34.110 --> 00:47:37.973
There was a European group this week,

00:47:39.330 --> 00:47:41.340
I read this in The
Financial Times I think,

00:47:41.340 --> 00:47:46.080
did a report making exactly
suggestions like you just did

00:47:47.070 --> 00:47:51.840
suggesting that there
should be various ways

00:47:51.840 --> 00:47:55.120
to do a lot of these
things in a greener way.

00:47:55.120 --> 00:47:57.380
I think what it would require

00:47:57.380 --> 00:48:01.690
is countries in the region

00:48:01.690 --> 00:48:05.253
also saying we just have
to have greener projects.

00:48:07.280 --> 00:48:09.380
The dams that China built,

00:48:09.380 --> 00:48:11.830
not only in China, but also several

00:48:13.844 --> 00:48:18.844
in Laos and in Cambodia just really are

00:48:20.386 --> 00:48:22.486
ecologically very devastating and harmful.

00:48:23.344 --> 00:48:28.344
But I think you also have the
benefit of China back home

00:48:28.780 --> 00:48:32.533
facing a lot of pressure from environment,

00:48:34.113 --> 00:48:35.580
just the population getting tired

00:48:35.580 --> 00:48:37.913
of having dirty hair, dirty water,

00:48:39.180 --> 00:48:43.970
and wanting more
environmentally sound projects.

00:48:43.970 --> 00:48:48.120
And it's interesting that
China now is only exporting

00:48:48.120 --> 00:48:52.610
really its worst coal fired plants

00:48:52.610 --> 00:48:54.881
to neighboring countries.

00:48:54.881 --> 00:48:56.630
They no longer can get away
with doing this at home.

00:48:56.630 --> 00:49:00.050
So I think if some of the tensions

00:49:00.050 --> 00:49:01.380
were brought down a little bit

00:49:01.380 --> 00:49:04.020
that maybe the US and China

00:49:04.020 --> 00:49:07.770
could get back on the same page on,

00:49:07.770 --> 00:49:12.510
sort of on the same page under
the Paris Climate Agreement,

00:49:12.510 --> 00:49:16.420
I think there would be ways to press China

00:49:16.420 --> 00:49:19.190
at the highest international levels,

00:49:19.190 --> 00:49:23.100
but then also working with countries.

00:49:23.100 --> 00:49:25.050
Laos, for example,

00:49:25.050 --> 00:49:30.050
they're now trying to build
one more dam on the Mekong.

00:49:30.320 --> 00:49:33.690
And I don't know where they
get off on these things.

00:49:33.690 --> 00:49:35.680
These things cost two billion dollars.

00:49:35.680 --> 00:49:36.870
They have to repay this.

00:49:36.870 --> 00:49:38.570
And I didn't even go into the story

00:49:38.570 --> 00:49:43.570
where the electricity
distribution company, Laos,

00:49:44.010 --> 00:49:46.583
in September had to turn over,

00:49:50.070 --> 00:49:53.150
because of its massive
debt to the Chinese,

00:49:53.150 --> 00:49:55.870
had to allow China Southern Power

00:49:55.870 --> 00:49:58.240
to take over their power grid,

00:49:58.240 --> 00:50:00.420
which is just unbelievable.

00:50:00.420 --> 00:50:04.170
And I can't believe that
the international community,

00:50:04.170 --> 00:50:06.320
the IMF offered other solutions,

00:50:06.320 --> 00:50:09.550
but in the end they just did this

00:50:09.550 --> 00:50:10.870
they went with the Chinese.

00:50:10.870 --> 00:50:12.727
But I can't believe

00:50:12.727 --> 00:50:15.920
that Thailand and Vietnam, among others,

00:50:15.920 --> 00:50:18.130
wouldn't have just said,
no, we're not doing this.

00:50:18.130 --> 00:50:20.740
We'll figure out ways to bail you out.

00:50:20.740 --> 00:50:23.297
But then they want to
build another dam on the,

00:50:24.262 --> 00:50:26.173
which the Thai's are already saying,

00:50:27.050 --> 00:50:27.970
that's the other thing,

00:50:27.970 --> 00:50:31.240
you can get neighboring
countries to put pressure on.

00:50:31.240 --> 00:50:35.330
So the Thai's after a dam burst

00:50:35.330 --> 00:50:38.090
in Southern Laos two years ago,

00:50:38.090 --> 00:50:40.210
unfortunately it wasn't
built by the Chinese,

00:50:40.210 --> 00:50:41.910
it was built by the South Koreans.

00:50:42.790 --> 00:50:45.163
We needed that lever,
but we didn't get it.

00:50:48.240 --> 00:50:49.780
The Thai population is saying,

00:50:49.780 --> 00:50:53.510
we no longer want hydropower electricity.

00:50:53.510 --> 00:50:55.940
Laos has lots of land, lots of mountains,

00:50:55.940 --> 00:50:58.680
lots of wind, lots of sun.

00:50:58.680 --> 00:51:00.310
Solar is going down,

00:51:00.310 --> 00:51:01.790
the cost of solar is going down,

00:51:01.790 --> 00:51:03.180
the cost of wind's going down.

00:51:03.180 --> 00:51:05.410
Why don't we just introduce that stuff?

00:51:05.410 --> 00:51:07.100
And we will buy your power.

00:51:07.100 --> 00:51:10.340
And I think if we put pressure
on countries like Laos

00:51:10.340 --> 00:51:13.120
and put pressure on the
Chinese internationally

00:51:13.120 --> 00:51:14.870
and in the region,

00:51:14.870 --> 00:51:18.443
I think you could get quite a
bit of traction to move ahead.

00:51:21.150 --> 00:51:22.800
But it would need a lot of persistence

00:51:22.800 --> 00:51:25.393
and coordination, I think, yeah.

00:51:26.240 --> 00:51:27.400
Good question.

00:51:27.400 --> 00:51:28.233
- Thank you, Murray.

00:51:28.233 --> 00:51:30.010
I'm gonna turn it over to Gregory Miller,

00:51:30.010 --> 00:51:31.730
who has the next question.

00:51:31.730 --> 00:51:32.636
- Thank you.

00:51:32.636 --> 00:51:35.910
Back in 2017, after the APEC meeting,

00:51:35.910 --> 00:51:38.367
Compass in Jakarta ran a front page story,

00:51:38.367 --> 00:51:41.280
"Trump versus Xi, Xi Wins".

00:51:41.280 --> 00:51:42.220
And from what you've said,

00:51:42.220 --> 00:51:45.170
it sounds like that
hasn't changed very much

00:51:45.170 --> 00:51:47.633
nor did the Trump
administration seem to care.

00:51:48.580 --> 00:51:50.003
Should the US care,

00:51:51.410 --> 00:51:52.360
whether we should or shouldn't,

00:51:52.360 --> 00:51:56.180
what advice would you give
to the Biden administration

00:51:58.560 --> 00:52:01.193
to enhance US influence in Southeast Asia?

00:52:02.650 --> 00:52:03.483
- Yeah.

00:52:05.120 --> 00:52:06.300
Well you know,

00:52:06.300 --> 00:52:08.620
it's a little hard to make recommendations

00:52:08.620 --> 00:52:11.930
when you know that
Biden's first priorities

00:52:11.930 --> 00:52:14.010
have to be to get control of COVID

00:52:14.010 --> 00:52:16.460
and get this economy going again, right,

00:52:16.460 --> 00:52:18.370
for so many that are just locked out

00:52:20.000 --> 00:52:23.840
thanks to COVID and the
shutdown that related from it.

00:52:23.840 --> 00:52:28.080
Yeah, Southeast Asia
really does want the US

00:52:28.080 --> 00:52:29.690
to play a more active role.

00:52:29.690 --> 00:52:31.950
And when the US,

00:52:31.950 --> 00:52:34.470
when Congress passed
the so-called Build Act

00:52:34.470 --> 00:52:37.173
about what two or three
years ago, $60 billion,

00:52:38.360 --> 00:52:41.230
and then set up this thing called,

00:52:41.230 --> 00:52:43.020
they turned the old OPEC

00:52:43.020 --> 00:52:47.468
into something called the
Development Finance Corp,

00:52:47.468 --> 00:52:50.947
which obviously doesn't
have a lot of money,

00:52:50.947 --> 00:52:54.200
it's around 60 billion,
but it's at least a start.

00:52:54.200 --> 00:52:57.820
And they're able to do joint ventures

00:52:57.820 --> 00:53:01.530
with other countries like
the Japanese or us, even say.

00:53:01.530 --> 00:53:04.400
They're also able to do joint ventures

00:53:04.400 --> 00:53:06.066
with private companies.

00:53:06.066 --> 00:53:11.066
So that might be a possible
way to get involved.

00:53:11.200 --> 00:53:13.470
They were really bothered

00:53:13.470 --> 00:53:15.350
when the US pulled out of the,

00:53:15.350 --> 00:53:18.050
when Trump pulled out of the
Transpacific Partnership.

00:53:19.200 --> 00:53:20.740
It's not like that was gonna be

00:53:20.740 --> 00:53:24.110
the mother of all platforms to trade on,

00:53:24.110 --> 00:53:26.277
but it showed the US was involved

00:53:26.277 --> 00:53:29.760
and it provided to Vietnam and Malaysia,

00:53:29.760 --> 00:53:33.520
at least some alternatives
to just depending so heavily

00:53:33.520 --> 00:53:35.513
on China.

00:53:37.170 --> 00:53:38.580
They were very disappointed.

00:53:38.580 --> 00:53:42.230
Trump only showed up at one
East Asia Summit meeting.

00:53:42.230 --> 00:53:44.810
This is something that
follows immediately after

00:53:44.810 --> 00:53:49.810
the ASEAN, the November
ASEAN meeting of leaders.

00:53:52.410 --> 00:53:54.621
They do have the East Asia Summit.

00:53:54.621 --> 00:53:56.890
Trump showed up once
actually only in Manila.

00:53:56.890 --> 00:53:57.810
He gave a speech

00:53:57.810 --> 00:54:00.400
and left before the
meeting actually started.

00:54:00.400 --> 00:54:02.120
So they remember this stuff.

00:54:02.120 --> 00:54:03.270
For Southeast Asians,

00:54:03.270 --> 00:54:08.020
you know you show you're
interested by showing up

00:54:08.020 --> 00:54:11.900
and the Trump guys, they didn't
show up this year, again,

00:54:11.900 --> 00:54:14.180
but it was after the election,

00:54:14.180 --> 00:54:17.710
but they sent to the ASEAN Summit

00:54:17.710 --> 00:54:19.220
and to the East Asia Summit,

00:54:19.220 --> 00:54:20.250
they sent O'Brien,

00:54:20.250 --> 00:54:22.050
the head of the National Security Council,

00:54:22.050 --> 00:54:25.170
an unconfirmed member of the Trump team,

00:54:25.170 --> 00:54:28.910
which is not the highest level.

00:54:28.910 --> 00:54:30.470
The year before they sent him,

00:54:30.470 --> 00:54:32.253
and what's his name?

00:54:33.908 --> 00:54:37.650
Ross, the Secretary of Commerce,

00:54:37.650 --> 00:54:40.710
who also isn't the most dynamic secretary

00:54:40.710 --> 00:54:42.363
in Trump's cabinet.

00:54:44.627 --> 00:54:46.203
So they really,

00:54:48.470 --> 00:54:53.470
the Trump team has really
not shown Southeast Asia,

00:54:54.530 --> 00:54:56.180
except for the Pentagon,

00:54:56.180 --> 00:54:58.730
the Pentagon under Mattis initially,

00:54:58.730 --> 00:55:01.620
but continuing has showed up.

00:55:01.620 --> 00:55:03.190
It did a lot of exercises,

00:55:03.190 --> 00:55:04.440
it did a lot of training,

00:55:04.440 --> 00:55:07.977
it kept going with the exercises

00:55:10.840 --> 00:55:13.940
that have been going on for decades

00:55:15.376 --> 00:55:18.720
and tried to be helpful

00:55:18.720 --> 00:55:23.720
to countries like Vietnam,
Malaysia, Indonesia, Philippines,

00:55:24.530 --> 00:55:26.333
that have been wanting to,

00:55:27.470 --> 00:55:30.060
that really don't have the wherewithal

00:55:30.060 --> 00:55:33.130
to see what China's doing
in the South China Sea.

00:55:33.130 --> 00:55:36.670
So beginning under

00:55:36.670 --> 00:55:40.220
the last few years of
the Obama administration,

00:55:40.220 --> 00:55:42.060
the military start providing them

00:55:42.060 --> 00:55:46.846
with more maritime domain awareness,

00:55:46.846 --> 00:55:51.846
radar, small boats, 25, 30 foot
vessels that they could use

00:55:53.320 --> 00:55:55.913
to try to figure out what
the Chinese were up to.

00:55:58.080 --> 00:56:01.660
So I think they need to get
more involved economically.

00:56:01.660 --> 00:56:05.040
And if they could get involved
in some infrastructure,

00:56:05.040 --> 00:56:06.480
that would be good.

00:56:06.480 --> 00:56:07.990
And they are.

00:56:07.990 --> 00:56:09.460
They've just bid on,

00:56:09.460 --> 00:56:13.690
the US companies through
this vehicle, the DFC,

00:56:13.690 --> 00:56:16.770
have been bidding on
some projects in Myanmar

00:56:18.124 --> 00:56:19.773
on renewable energy.

00:56:20.975 --> 00:56:25.620
But they're gonna have to be more active.

00:56:25.620 --> 00:56:28.810
And the one thing that the
Southeast Asians don't want

00:56:28.810 --> 00:56:31.580
is an all out war, hot war,

00:56:31.580 --> 00:56:35.580
but even a cold war between
China and Southeast Asia,

00:56:35.580 --> 00:56:39.180
because they provide so much input

00:56:39.180 --> 00:56:41.210
to the global supply chain, right?

00:56:41.210 --> 00:56:45.070
So Apple, Apple iPhones,

00:56:45.070 --> 00:56:48.650
or a lot of the computers
are assembled in China

00:56:48.650 --> 00:56:52.210
using technology from
all over Southeast Asia.

00:56:52.210 --> 00:56:56.577
So they would not like to see that either.

00:56:56.577 --> 00:56:58.690
But yeah, get involved.

00:56:58.690 --> 00:57:00.240
And I think Biden's people,

00:57:00.240 --> 00:57:03.134
the people he's putting in the White House

00:57:03.134 --> 00:57:06.575
and in the state department,

00:57:06.575 --> 00:57:08.610
assuming they get confirmed,

00:57:08.610 --> 00:57:11.107
are people that are more engagers

00:57:11.107 --> 00:57:14.393
than they the guys who were under Trump.

00:57:15.700 --> 00:57:17.260
- Thank you.

00:57:17.260 --> 00:57:18.639
- Thanks, Murray.

00:57:18.639 --> 00:57:22.211
We have one more question
from Daniel Yonto,

00:57:22.211 --> 00:57:23.810
sorry about that.

00:57:23.810 --> 00:57:24.740
Before we turn to that,

00:57:24.740 --> 00:57:27.130
I should note that it is one o'clock.

00:57:27.130 --> 00:57:29.060
Those of you who needed to leave at one,

00:57:29.060 --> 00:57:30.220
you're free to do so,

00:57:30.220 --> 00:57:32.230
but we will keep the conversation going

00:57:32.230 --> 00:57:34.813
for another 10, 15 minutes.

00:57:34.813 --> 00:57:38.390
I have time for that and
I hope you do as well.

00:57:38.390 --> 00:57:41.130
Murray, do you have time to
stay for an extra 15 minutes?

00:57:41.130 --> 00:57:42.211
- Yes.

00:57:42.211 --> 00:57:43.044
- [Erik] Okay, great.

00:57:43.044 --> 00:57:43.877
Go ahead, Daniel.

00:57:43.877 --> 00:57:44.710
- Hi Murray.

00:57:44.710 --> 00:57:46.810
I really do appreciate the
country by country analysis.

00:57:46.810 --> 00:57:48.577
I thought it was really fascinating.

00:57:48.577 --> 00:57:51.580
However, you've been
briefly mentioning ASEAN

00:57:51.580 --> 00:57:54.740
and I'm curious to what
extent ASEAN countries

00:57:54.740 --> 00:57:56.630
sort of as a collective unit

00:57:56.630 --> 00:57:59.253
have been able to influence
China in the region?

00:58:01.570 --> 00:58:04.100
- Yeah, that is another whole topic

00:58:04.100 --> 00:58:05.733
I should maybe have gone into.

00:58:07.160 --> 00:58:12.160
So one thing that has
really hurt ASEAN's ability

00:58:14.250 --> 00:58:15.500
to stand up to China

00:58:15.500 --> 00:58:19.770
is that you need to have
unanimity within the grouping

00:58:19.770 --> 00:58:24.770
before you can issue
communiques, for example.

00:58:25.380 --> 00:58:28.150
So beginning in 2012,

00:58:28.150 --> 00:58:31.230
you might recall that there was a,

00:58:31.230 --> 00:58:34.870
that was the year that China
had taken Scarborough Shoal

00:58:34.870 --> 00:58:36.962
from the Philippines.

00:58:36.962 --> 00:58:40.020
ASEAN tried to issue a statement

00:58:40.020 --> 00:58:44.550
and Cambodia was hosting ASEAN that year,

00:58:44.550 --> 00:58:49.487
and Cambodia by far is the
country closest to China.

00:58:53.190 --> 00:58:55.660
And they had Chinese officials

00:58:57.258 --> 00:58:59.450
in some of the visiting rooms

00:58:59.450 --> 00:59:02.149
close to where the meeting was happening.

00:59:02.149 --> 00:59:06.060
And Hor Namhong, who was
then the foreign minister,

00:59:06.060 --> 00:59:09.013
probably one of Ben's
favorite friends, Hor Namhong,

00:59:09.013 --> 00:59:09.846
no I'm kidding,

00:59:09.846 --> 00:59:13.770
but he would constantly
go check with the Chinese

00:59:13.770 --> 00:59:17.540
and in the end, they couldn't
come up with a resolution.

00:59:17.540 --> 00:59:22.540
And that really has
hurt the ASEAN's a lot.

00:59:25.100 --> 00:59:30.100
Almost every year, there's
an effort at bringing some

00:59:32.359 --> 00:59:37.270
just joint statement to
criticize China for its behavior

00:59:37.270 --> 00:59:38.720
and they can't do it

00:59:38.720 --> 00:59:41.380
because of China's ability to split off

00:59:42.737 --> 00:59:44.810
always Cambodia

00:59:44.810 --> 00:59:48.070
and quite often Laos
and quite often Myanmar.

00:59:48.070 --> 00:59:50.210
Now the thing that's gonna be interesting

00:59:50.210 --> 00:59:52.690
is what's gonna happen in the Mekong.

00:59:52.690 --> 00:59:56.930
This thing has really been
heating up for most of 2020.

00:59:56.930 --> 01:00:01.200
And Vietnam tried to,

01:00:01.200 --> 01:00:03.480
I understand from Vietnamese diplomats

01:00:03.480 --> 01:00:05.253
that they tried to bring,

01:00:06.340 --> 01:00:09.092
raise this issue in ASEAN

01:00:09.092 --> 01:00:11.870
thinking that maybe they
would get more support

01:00:11.870 --> 01:00:15.330
from countries like Cambodia,
Laos, and even Thailand,

01:00:15.330 --> 01:00:17.480
that doesn't care much
about the South China Sea,

01:00:17.480 --> 01:00:20.367
'cause it isn't a disputing country.

01:00:20.367 --> 01:00:23.660
But it really didn't work.

01:00:23.660 --> 01:00:25.240
But it's really interesting.

01:00:25.240 --> 01:00:27.150
There is something called,

01:00:27.150 --> 01:00:28.560
let's see if I can recreate it now,

01:00:28.560 --> 01:00:29.720
I don't use this word every,

01:00:29.720 --> 01:00:31.760
this acronym every day,

01:00:31.760 --> 01:00:36.760
but there is a UN convention

01:00:36.800 --> 01:00:41.800
on non navigable uses of
rivers or something like that,

01:00:43.380 --> 01:00:45.100
I don't know exactly what it's called,

01:00:45.100 --> 01:00:50.070
but there are 18 countries in Asia,

01:00:50.070 --> 01:00:51.550
not only Southeast Asia,

01:00:51.550 --> 01:00:54.780
that for whom the source
of their river begins

01:00:54.780 --> 01:00:58.450
in China.

01:00:58.450 --> 01:01:02.790
And if countries that are losing water

01:01:02.790 --> 01:01:06.800
because of China's taking it,

01:01:06.800 --> 01:01:08.625
keeping it, storing it,

01:01:08.625 --> 01:01:13.330
would all, gang up
maybe is the right word,

01:01:13.330 --> 01:01:17.250
maybe if they'd all cooperate
and put pressure on China,

01:01:17.250 --> 01:01:20.270
not only a couple of little
countries in Southeast Asia,

01:01:20.270 --> 01:01:23.200
but also Central Asia, South Asia,

01:01:23.200 --> 01:01:26.310
you might be able to get China

01:01:27.291 --> 01:01:30.807
to recognize, yeah if China says,

01:01:30.807 --> 01:01:32.270
"If it begins when our country,

01:01:32.270 --> 01:01:33.990
we have rights of first use.

01:01:33.990 --> 01:01:36.190
That's your problem if
you're further down."

01:01:39.420 --> 01:01:40.500
I think China,

01:01:40.500 --> 01:01:42.970
if it wants to be a global player

01:01:42.970 --> 01:01:44.820
is going to probably have to learn

01:01:44.820 --> 01:01:47.610
how to share its toys in the sandbox.

01:01:47.610 --> 01:01:52.610
And I think that that,

01:01:52.990 --> 01:01:56.980
the Mekong does open up
a whole new opportunity

01:01:59.090 --> 01:02:01.970
for getting countries like Cambodia,

01:02:01.970 --> 01:02:03.950
if Cambodian farmers,

01:02:03.950 --> 01:02:07.372
so China gives Cambodia and
Laos all this aid, right?

01:02:07.372 --> 01:02:11.540
But if the cost to their farmers

01:02:11.540 --> 01:02:13.720
in rice and fish

01:02:13.720 --> 01:02:17.190
is far more than what they
get from China in aid,

01:02:17.190 --> 01:02:19.140
are they gonna want to keep doing this?

01:02:20.467 --> 01:02:21.300
It doesn't make any sense.

01:02:21.300 --> 01:02:23.170
So let's see what happens.

01:02:23.170 --> 01:02:25.520
But ASEAN,

01:02:25.520 --> 01:02:28.770
because it's based on consensus

01:02:28.770 --> 01:02:30.940
has a lot of trouble

01:02:30.940 --> 01:02:34.570
putting a lot of pressure on
countries unless they can,

01:02:34.570 --> 01:02:35.963
everybody agrees.

01:02:37.200 --> 01:02:38.250
- Thank you for that.

01:02:39.290 --> 01:02:40.750
- Great.

01:02:40.750 --> 01:02:42.253
Our next question is from David Lindsay.

01:02:45.760 --> 01:02:46.993
- Yes, am I un-muted?

01:02:48.030 --> 01:02:49.396
- [Erik] Yes.

01:02:49.396 --> 01:02:50.229
- Can you?
- Yes.

01:02:50.229 --> 01:02:51.570
- Thank you very much for this talk.

01:02:51.570 --> 01:02:53.230
It's fascinating.

01:02:53.230 --> 01:02:55.900
And I want to ask you about

01:02:57.870 --> 01:03:00.950
something I heard from
a scholar on Vietnam

01:03:00.950 --> 01:03:04.840
in this same lecture
series a few years ago.

01:03:04.840 --> 01:03:08.330
He reported that many Chinese vessels

01:03:08.330 --> 01:03:13.140
were sinking Vietnamese fishing boats.

01:03:13.140 --> 01:03:16.020
It was 2018 or '19.

01:03:16.020 --> 01:03:19.770
And he said that the problem

01:03:19.770 --> 01:03:21.150
was that the Vietnamese government,

01:03:21.150 --> 01:03:22.710
the higher levels,

01:03:22.710 --> 01:03:26.370
were now basically on the
Chinese government payroll,

01:03:26.370 --> 01:03:27.610
they had been bought out

01:03:27.610 --> 01:03:31.042
and so they were not
raising a fuss about this

01:03:31.042 --> 01:03:32.233
and that it was,

01:03:33.275 --> 01:03:36.760
there were actually
demonstrations in the streets

01:03:36.760 --> 01:03:41.560
by fishermen and their
friends and patriots,

01:03:41.560 --> 01:03:43.810
but that the Chinese government

01:03:43.810 --> 01:03:46.300
was turning a blind eye.

01:03:46.300 --> 01:03:48.680
Could you comment on
whether there's some truth

01:03:48.680 --> 01:03:50.123
to this story or not?

01:03:52.570 --> 01:03:56.090
- So Vietnamese fishermen,

01:03:56.090 --> 01:03:58.170
there are two big groupings of islands

01:03:58.170 --> 01:03:59.040
in the South China Sea.

01:03:59.040 --> 01:04:01.053
The Northern one is the Paracel's,

01:04:03.130 --> 01:04:07.650
which the Chinese took from
the Vietnamese in 1974.

01:04:07.650 --> 01:04:10.123
You might remember in 1974,

01:04:11.007 --> 01:04:13.157
the US backed South Vietnamese government

01:04:13.157 --> 01:04:14.940
was still in power.

01:04:14.940 --> 01:04:16.810
And they took it from that government,

01:04:16.810 --> 01:04:18.110
the US let 'em do it

01:04:19.142 --> 01:04:22.023
'cause they were in the middle
of normalizing relations.

01:04:22.880 --> 01:04:25.730
But now it's really too late.

01:04:25.730 --> 01:04:28.430
The Chinese have now
controlled it for years.

01:04:28.430 --> 01:04:31.210
But a lot of fishermen,
Vietnamese fishermen,

01:04:31.210 --> 01:04:34.010
from central and Northern Vietnam go there

01:04:34.010 --> 01:04:37.050
and they are regularly arrested,

01:04:37.050 --> 01:04:40.730
some boats are burned, some are sunk, yes.

01:04:40.730 --> 01:04:44.810
They also think boats of the
Filipinos and Indonesians

01:04:44.810 --> 01:04:46.143
off their coasts.

01:04:47.032 --> 01:04:49.600
Every year, China,

01:04:49.600 --> 01:04:51.800
so China puts in force

01:04:51.800 --> 01:04:55.180
a moratorium that divides
the South China Sea

01:04:55.180 --> 01:04:58.650
and any fishing north
of that line is banned.

01:04:58.650 --> 01:05:01.863
It's at the 35th parallel
I think if I recall.

01:05:03.620 --> 01:05:07.033
And so they do detain, sink,

01:05:07.974 --> 01:05:12.730
more detain and then release
after awhile, fishermen.

01:05:12.730 --> 01:05:14.920
But there are some
sinking, that's for sure.

01:05:14.920 --> 01:05:18.400
Now domestically, Vietnam has,

01:05:18.400 --> 01:05:22.210
Hanoi has a really interesting situation.

01:05:22.210 --> 01:05:26.610
In 2018 Vietnam in the
middle of the year, Vietnam,

01:05:26.610 --> 01:05:30.490
the national assembly of
Vietnam tried to pass a law

01:05:30.490 --> 01:05:32.803
on special economic zones.

01:05:34.020 --> 01:05:37.490
That law didn't mention China once.

01:05:37.490 --> 01:05:41.490
But the population assumed
that Chinese companies

01:05:41.490 --> 01:05:43.580
were gonna get access to land.

01:05:43.580 --> 01:05:47.970
And the laws said that
a company that invests

01:05:47.970 --> 01:05:50.950
could keep get keep the land for 99 years.

01:05:50.950 --> 01:05:52.270
Well, Vietnamese farmers

01:05:52.270 --> 01:05:54.300
can only keep their land for 50 years

01:05:54.300 --> 01:05:56.950
and then they have to
renegotiate and all that stuff.

01:05:58.630 --> 01:06:00.490
So the population went crazy

01:06:00.490 --> 01:06:03.080
and really did have huge protests,

01:06:03.080 --> 01:06:07.200
both in Hanoi and in the
South and in central Vietnam.

01:06:07.200 --> 01:06:08.783
Some of them turned violent.

01:06:09.634 --> 01:06:14.634
At a Taiwanese steel mill
in North central Vietnam,

01:06:16.250 --> 01:06:19.540
in Tai Ning, in Ting Wa rather, they

01:06:23.130 --> 01:06:24.210
attacked the steel mill

01:06:24.210 --> 01:06:25.990
that was being built by Chinese workers

01:06:25.990 --> 01:06:27.750
for this Taiwanese company.

01:06:27.750 --> 01:06:32.750
And two or three or so Chinese
actually died in the process.

01:06:34.840 --> 01:06:39.840
The government recognizes
fully that it has,

01:06:40.740 --> 01:06:43.713
it's on a very short
leash by the population.

01:06:45.070 --> 01:06:49.580
In 19, let me see now, 1997,

01:06:49.580 --> 01:06:53.970
the government gave a gypsum
mine in central Vietnam

01:06:53.970 --> 01:06:58.970
to the Chinese and even
General Vo Nguyen Giap,

01:06:59.560 --> 01:07:02.800
the general who helped win the war

01:07:02.800 --> 01:07:04.703
for the Vietnamese communists,

01:07:06.341 --> 01:07:07.741
he wrote a letter protesting

01:07:09.970 --> 01:07:14.360
giving the Chinese access to
this mine in central Vietnam

01:07:16.580 --> 01:07:18.723
in the Highlands, central Highlands.

01:07:19.760 --> 01:07:20.900
There is yeah,

01:07:20.900 --> 01:07:24.123
and you have academics,

01:07:27.123 --> 01:07:28.103
a lot of artists,

01:07:29.960 --> 01:07:31.910
lot of people protests

01:07:31.910 --> 01:07:35.533
almost anything that the
Vietnamese government does.

01:07:36.470 --> 01:07:39.760
Is the government sold out to China?

01:07:39.760 --> 01:07:40.880
No.

01:07:40.880 --> 01:07:43.783
They are standing up to China a lot.

01:07:46.500 --> 01:07:50.213
Sometimes they capitulate,
but the Vietnamese,

01:07:51.572 --> 01:07:53.380
I got quite a bit of
access in doing this book

01:07:53.380 --> 01:07:56.130
to party officials.

01:07:56.130 --> 01:08:01.070
And they said that in 2018,

01:08:04.040 --> 01:08:06.870
when China was putting a lot of pressure

01:08:06.870 --> 01:08:10.823
on this Spanish oil company called Repsol,

01:08:13.020 --> 01:08:16.360
the politburo voted,
I'm trying to remember,

01:08:16.360 --> 01:08:18.883
the politburo was 17?

01:08:20.106 --> 01:08:20.939
Do you remember, Erik?

01:08:20.939 --> 01:08:22.390
Is the politburo, 17 or 19 now?

01:08:22.390 --> 01:08:24.060
They've increased the numbers.

01:08:24.060 --> 01:08:24.940
I can't remember,

01:08:24.940 --> 01:08:27.800
but it's somewhere either 17 or 19.

01:08:27.800 --> 01:08:32.800
Two officials voted to abandon
the project with Repsol,

01:08:33.530 --> 01:08:36.090
which cost the Vietnamese $500 million.

01:08:36.090 --> 01:08:38.610
They owed Repsol around $500 million.

01:08:38.610 --> 01:08:40.380
Neither side has said exactly,

01:08:40.380 --> 01:08:43.280
but some economists have calculated it

01:08:43.280 --> 01:08:44.680
to have cost them that much.

01:08:45.840 --> 01:08:50.680
The party chief and the
head of the military

01:08:50.680 --> 01:08:52.570
voted to abandon the project.

01:08:52.570 --> 01:08:56.700
The rest of the poliburo
voted to stand their ground.

01:08:56.700 --> 01:09:01.040
But the two leaders did win in that case

01:09:01.040 --> 01:09:02.720
and they abandoned the project.

01:09:02.720 --> 01:09:07.350
However, when China repeated
this pressure last year,

01:09:07.350 --> 01:09:10.820
the Vietnamese no longer
had a Spanish company,

01:09:10.820 --> 01:09:11.720
they had Repsol,

01:09:11.720 --> 01:09:16.720
which is a Vladimir
Putin company enterprise,

01:09:17.070 --> 01:09:19.370
and they don't stand down easily

01:09:19.370 --> 01:09:21.520
for anybody, as you would guess.

01:09:21.520 --> 01:09:23.300
And they didn't leave.

01:09:23.300 --> 01:09:27.763
And in the end, the Chinese
just sort of wore out and left.

01:09:28.870 --> 01:09:30.750
It's a very complicated issue.

01:09:30.750 --> 01:09:32.510
But to say that Vietnam,

01:09:32.510 --> 01:09:35.793
Vietnam is taking no Belt and Road money.

01:09:36.890 --> 01:09:40.900
China has included a very
disastrous project in it

01:09:40.900 --> 01:09:43.593
that started actually well
before the Belt and Road.

01:09:45.490 --> 01:09:48.240
It's the sky train in Hanoi,

01:09:48.240 --> 01:09:50.830
which has been built under
construction for 10 years.

01:09:50.830 --> 01:09:51.970
The price keeps going up

01:09:51.970 --> 01:09:54.784
and it's never gonna be finished, I guess,

01:09:54.784 --> 01:09:57.653
if you're cynical, but,

01:10:02.050 --> 01:10:06.840
the Vietnamese are doing the
best they can, I would say,

01:10:06.840 --> 01:10:10.060
to stand up to China without going to war,

01:10:10.060 --> 01:10:12.133
which they know they can't afford.

01:10:13.548 --> 01:10:15.980
I don't know if that
answers your question.

01:10:15.980 --> 01:10:17.210
I know it's rambling,

01:10:17.210 --> 01:10:20.470
but it's a very complicated
issue in Vietnam.

01:10:20.470 --> 01:10:21.893
- No, that's great.

01:10:21.893 --> 01:10:22.726
Thank you.

01:10:22.726 --> 01:10:23.559
- I'll just add Murray.

01:10:23.559 --> 01:10:26.660
I think you were definitely
right on the money

01:10:26.660 --> 01:10:28.415
with your answer there.

01:10:28.415 --> 01:10:32.770
Also with the recent
politburo change, it was 2016.

01:10:32.770 --> 01:10:35.423
I think it's actually the 12th politburo,

01:10:35.423 --> 01:10:38.960
but there was a shuffling.

01:10:38.960 --> 01:10:43.960
And I think some of the
consolidation of power that occurred

01:10:44.030 --> 01:10:49.030
used claims of the previous
prime minister Vo Nguyen,

01:10:49.473 --> 01:10:52.290
claiming that he was in
the pocket of the Chinese.

01:10:52.290 --> 01:10:53.550
Whether he really was or not,

01:10:53.550 --> 01:10:54.870
it's a very complicated thing

01:10:54.870 --> 01:10:57.030
to figure out to be honest,

01:10:57.030 --> 01:10:59.410
but they certainly
mobilized claims about that

01:10:59.410 --> 01:11:01.810
in justification to remove him.

01:11:01.810 --> 01:11:06.100
And the current leadership
would certainly portray itself

01:11:08.750 --> 01:11:11.310
as strong against China.

01:11:11.310 --> 01:11:15.190
We have one last question from,

01:11:15.190 --> 01:11:17.520
I think it was Anita Nai, was it?

01:11:17.520 --> 01:11:19.560
Anita, did you want me

01:11:19.560 --> 01:11:21.520
to read your question that you had posted

01:11:21.520 --> 01:11:23.770
or did you want to read
it out loud yourself?

01:11:25.670 --> 01:11:27.893
Or are you even still here?

01:11:32.610 --> 01:11:33.923
Anita, are you there?

01:11:36.580 --> 01:11:39.228
- She said, "Please go ahead", she writes.

01:11:39.228 --> 01:11:40.061
- Oh, please go ahead.

01:11:40.061 --> 01:11:41.240
I missed it, the chat disappeared.

01:11:41.240 --> 01:11:43.310
So I'll read it

01:11:43.310 --> 01:11:45.590
and you can see it
yourself as well, Murray,

01:11:45.590 --> 01:11:46.577
but I'll read it quickly.

01:11:46.577 --> 01:11:49.220
"Chinese FDI into the
US has dropped a lot.

01:11:49.220 --> 01:11:52.060
How has the trend been
into Southeast Asia?

01:11:52.060 --> 01:11:55.270
One key sector for the FDI is tech",

01:11:55.270 --> 01:11:57.477
and there's a list of tech companies,

01:11:57.477 --> 01:12:00.580
"And then with tension high
between Australia and China,

01:12:00.580 --> 01:12:02.630
has Australia been stepping
up their involvement

01:12:02.630 --> 01:12:04.330
with ASEAN affairs?"

01:12:04.330 --> 01:12:06.240
Given the time that we have,

01:12:06.240 --> 01:12:10.170
you can either answer both
of them quickly or briefly

01:12:11.910 --> 01:12:13.090
or one of them.

01:12:13.090 --> 01:12:17.400
- Yeah, well this year,

01:12:17.400 --> 01:12:20.320
obviously Chinese
investment has been hammered

01:12:20.320 --> 01:12:23.490
just because it's so hard to travel.

01:12:23.490 --> 01:12:27.353
There's no Chinese are going in and out.

01:12:29.056 --> 01:12:31.680
So it will be reduced this year,

01:12:31.680 --> 01:12:33.930
but I think generally,

01:12:33.930 --> 01:12:36.070
I would say Chinese investment

01:12:36.070 --> 01:12:38.835
has really started to take off.

01:12:38.835 --> 01:12:40.280
It was slow for a long time,

01:12:40.280 --> 01:12:42.350
but it's really started to take off.

01:12:42.350 --> 01:12:43.550
The Belt and Road,

01:12:43.550 --> 01:12:45.750
maybe it started very high,

01:12:45.750 --> 01:12:47.943
it's maybe going down,

01:12:49.060 --> 01:12:53.230
but general investment,
including in technology,

01:12:53.230 --> 01:12:57.556
as you said and as I noted,

01:12:57.556 --> 01:13:00.000
is still really going up.

01:13:00.000 --> 01:13:02.440
Australia, it's too early

01:13:02.440 --> 01:13:07.070
to really have seen
Australia investment go up

01:13:07.070 --> 01:13:12.070
as a result of the Wolf
Warriors going after Australia,

01:13:14.220 --> 01:13:16.370
but Australia has been,

01:13:16.370 --> 01:13:19.900
over the last oh two or three years,

01:13:19.900 --> 01:13:21.960
getting much more active
in Southeast Asia,

01:13:21.960 --> 01:13:25.120
cooperating with Japan and others

01:13:25.120 --> 01:13:28.410
to play a bigger role in
Southeast Asia, which is,

01:13:28.410 --> 01:13:29.760
as a middle power,

01:13:29.760 --> 01:13:33.173
Southeast Asia really
appreciates Australia's role.

01:13:37.440 --> 01:13:39.006
- Thank you, Murray.

01:13:39.006 --> 01:13:42.560
With that, I think we're at
the end of our allotted time.

01:13:42.560 --> 01:13:47.260
I want to thank you for such a
fascinating discussion today.

01:13:47.260 --> 01:13:49.610
Obviously you've opened so many doorways

01:13:49.610 --> 01:13:51.820
to further investigation as well,

01:13:51.820 --> 01:13:54.280
and I hope that this topic,

01:13:54.280 --> 01:13:56.703
we can keep revisiting during this series.

01:13:58.480 --> 01:13:59.670
Also it occurred to me

01:13:59.670 --> 01:14:02.070
that it would be very interesting
sometime in the future

01:14:02.070 --> 01:14:04.780
to organize a panel discussion,

01:14:04.780 --> 01:14:06.840
perhaps with you Nayan Chunda

01:14:06.840 --> 01:14:10.564
about the history of the
Far Eastern Economic Review

01:14:10.564 --> 01:14:14.570
or to hear about the
time of your journalism,

01:14:14.570 --> 01:14:16.140
which was such an important time

01:14:16.140 --> 01:14:19.600
in developing on the ground
knowledge about Southeast Asia.

01:14:19.600 --> 01:14:21.500
But we'll leave that for another time.

01:14:22.410 --> 01:14:26.580
I'll linger until people sign off

01:14:26.580 --> 01:14:29.190
and leave the chat open
for a couple more minutes.

01:14:29.190 --> 01:14:31.130
But I just wanted to thank you formerly

01:14:31.130 --> 01:14:33.480
and I'll press stop on
the recording right now.

